%S A398182 0,2,2,8,28,128,496,2048,8128,32768,130816,524288,2096128,8388608,
%T A398182 33550336,134217728,536854528,2147483648,8589869056,34359738368,
%U A398182 137438691328,549755813888,2199022206976,8796093022208,35184367894528,140737488355328,562949936644096,2251799813685248,9007199187632128
%N A398182 Degree of the minimal polynomial whose root is the sidelength of the largest n-cube that fits inside a unit (n+1)-cube.
%C A398182 Finding the embedded n-cube has been called the generalized Rupert problem. The Babylonians knew a(1). Nieuwland found a(2) over 100 years after John Wallis solved the original Rupert problem. DeVicci Shultz discovered a(3) in the late 20th century. The other values follow from the work of Huber and Ligocki. The minimal polynomial is sometimes called the Nieuwland polynomial, N_n(x). Some of the a(n) are perfect numbers (n=4,6,8,14,18,20,32,...). For n even, all a(n) are triangular numbers (except at n=2).
%D A398182 Hallard T. Croft, Kenneth Falconer and Richard K. Guy, Unsolved Problems in Geometry, Springer-Verlag New York, 1991, Problem B4, p. 53.
%D A398182 Steven R. Finch, Mathematical Constants, Cambridge University Press, 2003, Section 8.14, DeVicci's tesseract constant, p. 524.
%D A398182 Greg Huber and Terry J. Ligocki, Self-similarity, commensurability, and scaling in the generalized Rupert problem, unpublished (2026).
%D A398182 Greg Huber, Kay Pechenick Shultz and John E. Wetzel, The n-cube is Rupert, The American Mathematical Monthly, Vol. 125, No. 6 (2018), pp. 505-512.
%D A398182 Schrek, D. J. E. Prince Rupert's Problem and Its Extension by Pieter Nieuwland. Scripta Math. 16, 73-80 and 261-267, 1950.
%H A398182 Kay R. Pechenick DeVicci Shultz and Greg Huber, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20231019095801/https://www.kitp.ucsb.edu/sites/default/files/preprints/2013/13-142.pdf">Largest m-Cube in an n-Cube: Partial Solution</a>, KITP preprint, NSF-ITP-13-142.
%H A398182 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (4,4,-16).
%F A398182 For n even (n>2), a(n)=2^(n-2)*(2^(n-1)-1); for n odd (n>1), a(n)=2^(2*n-3).
%e A398182 For n=1, the minimal polynomial is x^2-2, since the unit square has diagonal sqrt(2).
%e A398182 For n=2, Pieter Nieuwland showed that the minimal polynomial is 8*x^2-9.
%e A398182 For n=3, Kay DeVicci Shultz found the minimal polynomial to be 4*x^8-28*x^6-7*x^4+16*x^2+16.
%t A398182 a[0]=0; a[1]=a[2]=2; a[n_]:=If[EvenQ[n],2^(n-2)*(2^(n-1)-1),2^(2*n-3)]; Array[a,30,0] (* _Stefano Spezia_, Jul 31 2026 *)
